<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">French Mauritania</h1><p class="st-meta">1903 CE – 1960 CE · capital Saint-Louis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">France's Saharan colony between Senegal and Morocco, governed for most of its history from Saint-Louis, outside its own borders.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>From 1902 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xavier-coppolani">Xavier Coppolani</a> pursued a policy of peaceful penetration, using alliances with religious leaders to bring the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-trarza">Emirate of Trarza</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-brakna">Emirate of Brakna</a> under French protection in 1903. After Coppolani's assassination in 1905, military campaigns, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-gouraud">Henri Gouraud's</a> conquest of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-adrar">Emirate of Adrar</a> in 1909, extended control northward.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> became a civil territory and, in 1920, a colony within <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa</a>, administered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-louis-senegal">Saint-Louis</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a>. French rule was light, relying on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, chiefs and religious leaders, and nomadic herding society changed slowly. Resistance in the far north continued into the 1930s, and investment was minimal.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1957 to 1958 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nouakchott">Nouakchott</a> as a new capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> became an autonomous Islamic Republic in 1958 and independent on 28 November 1960 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moktar-ould-daddah">Moktar Ould Daddah</a>, despite Moroccan claims to its territory.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1903 CE – 1960 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:97.57%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1903 CE – 1960 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saint-Louis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Xavier Coppolani, Henri Gouraud</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hassaniya Arabic, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Mauritania</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colony of Mauritania, Mauritanie</dd></dl></aside></div>

